# Build-time flags for the deployed site. Next.js reads this during
# `next build`, so what is here ships without anyone touching a dashboard.
#
# THIS FILE IS COMMITTED TO GIT. Never put a secret in it — no Turnstile secret,
# no service-role key, no pairing token. Those belong in the host's environment
# variables, which override anything set here.

# ─── Bot protection: OFF ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# TEMPORARY. This exists so the site can go up before there is a Cloudflare
# account, while only the doctor has the address and nobody else is testing.
#
# What still protects /demande: the caps inside submit_appointment_request()
# — 3 submissions per IP and 2 per phone, rolling 24h, enforced in the database
# so a caller cannot raise its own limit. That stops an accident, not a script.
# A script costs a proxy to get around it, and the price of losing is the
# secretary's inbox buried under junk she has to read before dismissing.
#
# Every submission logs "accepted with NO bot verification" while this is set,
# so the state shows up in the deployment logs rather than being forgotten.
#
# TO TURN PROTECTION BACK ON, before the address reaches a real patient:
#   1. dash.cloudflare.com → Turnstile → Add site, hostname = the live domain
#   2. put NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY and TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY in the host's
#      environment variables (the secret must NOT be prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_)
#   3. delete the line below
#   4. redeploy
NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_ENABLED=false
